.TH HOSTNAME 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hostname \- show or set the system host name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hostname
.RB [ \-f | \-s ]
.br
.B hostname
.B \-F
.I file
.br
.B hostname
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I name
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no operand,
.B hostname
prints the kernel host name.
By default only the part before the first dot is printed; use
.B \-f
to print the whole name.
No DNS lookup is performed in either case, and printing never changes
the name.
.PP
With a
.I name
operand,
.B hostname
sets the kernel host name to
.I name
with
.BR sethostname (2).
Only the superuser may set the name, and a name of 256 bytes or more is
refused.
.SH OPTIONS
Options are processed from left to right.
.TP
.B \-f
Print the full host name, including any domain part.
.TP
.B \-s
Print the short host name, up to the first dot (the default).
.TP
.BI \-F " file"
Set the host name from the first line of
.IR file ,
with trailing newline, carriage-return, space and tab characters
removed.
It is an error if the file is empty or the line is blank.
.B hostname
exits as soon as this option has been handled; any later arguments
are ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options; the next argument is taken as
.IR name .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown option was given,
.B \-F
had no argument,
.I file
could not be read or was blank, or getting or setting the host name
failed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/hostname
The configured host name, applied at boot by
.B /etc/rc.d/00-hostname
with
.BR \-F .
.SH EXAMPLES
Set the host name from the configuration file:
.PP
.EX
# hostname \-F /etc/hostname
.EE
.PP
Print the fully qualified name:
.PP
.EX
$ hostname \-f
build.example.org
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uname (1),
.BR sys_hostname (2),
.BR uname (2)
